AB  - AI coding agents operate tactically rather than strategically. While highly productive in isolated tasks, their limited context windows and behavioral unpredictability lead to technical debt accumulation and gradual architectural degradation. Consequently, large-scale production systems become increasingly difficult to maintain. We argue that reliable AIassisted development requires establishing architectural gravity: structural constraints that guide and stabilize agent behavior over time. We demonstrate this through a Design-First workflow that instruments a contract-driven development environment for full-stack systems. Domain models are transformed into production-ready backend APIs aligned with established patterns, while simultaneously generating constraint layers that embed system intent, reduce architectural drift, and preserve long-term maintainability. We evaluated this across system bootstrapping and complex feature extensions using both proprietary and open-weight LLMs. The results demonstrate that embedding architectural intent into workflow constraints enforces design goals automatically and preserves long-term maintainability. Notably, open-weight models achieved comparable architectural compliance to proprietary models under governance, indicating that well-structured constraints reduce dependence on frontier model capabilities. These findings suggest that reliable AI-assisted development is fundamentally an architectural governance challenge rather than a model capability problem.
