TI  - Self-improving multi-agent framework for zero-shot multimodal information extraction
T2  - EXPERT SYSTEMS WITH APPLICATIONS
LA  - English
KW  - Multimodal information extraction
KW  - Multi-agent system
KW  - Zero-shot learning
AB  - Zero-shot multimodal information extraction (MIE) aims to extract structured information from multimedia content without relying on annotated data, posing significant challenges due to minimal human intervention. Recent efforts adapt large language models (LLMs) to this task, leveraging their strong generalization capabilities. While promising, two key limitations remain: (i) most methods rely on single-turn or multi-turn interactions with a single LLM, which struggle with complex schemas, long input contexts, and nuanced instruction following; and (ii) demonstration retrieval is often based on shallow semantic similarity, neglecting entity-level relevance and misleading LLMs during inference. To address these challenges, we propose a self-improving multi-agent system (SIMAS) for zero-shot MIE. SIMAS comprises three specialized LLM-based agents: (i) a task-agnostic demonstration selector that retrieves entity-aware samples from unlabeled corpora, (ii) an machine reading comprehension (MRC)-based self-annotator that formulates annotation as a multiple-choice question answering (QA) task and employs a multi-agent debate (MD) mechanism for improved reliability, and (iii) a retrieval-augmented code generation predictor that reformulates MIE as structured code generation problems, guided by the self-annotated demonstrations. By distributing complex MIE problem across cooperative agents, SIMAS mitigates performance degradation from overloading a single agent and stimulates the self-learning capabilities of LLMs. Experiments on six benchmarks show that SIMAS consistently achieves state-of-the-art zero-shot performance, and remarkably, surpasses fine-tuned models trained on large-scale labeled data in three benchmarks.
