TI  - An LLM-based multi-agent framework for automated reliability analysis of spatially variable soils
T2  - ADVANCED ENGINEERING INFORMATICS
LA  - English
KW  - Large language model agents
KW  - Reliability analysis
KW  - Surrogate model
KW  - Spatial variability of soil
KW  - SLOPE STABILITY
AB  - Due to the inherent variability of soil properties, the Monte Carlo method is commonly used to conduct reliability analysis. To accelerate this process, time-consuming numerical simulations are often replaced with efficient surrogate models. However, current machine learning-based approaches for building these surrogates involve complex model construction, training, and hyperparameter tuning processes. Furthermore, accelerating reliability analysis via machine learning typically results in a fragmented workflow-requiring interaction with various software platforms, complex data transfers, and a heavy reliance on expert experience. Leveraging recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs), this paper proposes a hierarchical, LLM-based multi-agent framework for automated reliability analysis. We first demonstrate this approach using slope stability analysis as a primary example, and subsequently validate its generalization capabilities across other problems. The framework establishes a comprehensive workflow consisting of an Orchestrating Agent alongside three subagents responsible for dataset generation, surrogate model training, and large-scale prediction, respectively. The feasibility and accuracy of this framework have been validated across realistic scenarios of varying complexity. Experimental results demonstrate that the LLM-based multi-agent framework significantly reduces model construction time and minimizes the need for human intervention, while autonomously training accurate machine learning models. Additionally, this paper investigates the effects of self-correction, hyperparameter tuning, and prompt design on the performance of the proposed framework. The self-correction mechanism improves code-execution robustness, hyperparameter tuning enhances surrogate-model accuracy, and structured prompt design improves token and time efficiency during code generation. Ultimately, this study highlights the potential of LLM-based agents in advancing geotechnical engineering toward more intelligent and automated geotechnical reliability analysis.
