AB  - Context: Agentic coding systems are used in software maintenance workflows. As these systems move from isolated code generation toward repository-level change tasks, a question arises: which quality gates improve agent-produced maintenance artifacts, and which merely hide model, prompt, retry, and validation effects inside aggregate success rates? Objective: This study quantifies the role of quality gates as interventions. We examine how gate-feedback policies affect command-predicate success, accepted-artifact quality, rejection of invalid and valid artifacts, retry cost, and robustness across model sizes, while separating diagnostic content from retry opportunity. Method: We evaluate command-content acceptance without repository execution on TAM-Eval tasks using five Qwen2.5-Coder models from 1.5B to 32B parameters. The experiment evaluates five feedback policies on 100 adversarial tasks, with a complete 458-task paired setting, a matched generic-retry control, nested stratified 100-and 200-task samples, stochastic temperature/seed runs, and a clean-prompt control. We perform post-hoc gate ablation over 4359 archived software artifacts comprising real outputs and controlled rows. Results: The study contains 30,524 model-policy-task observations. Schema feedback does not improve over direct generation in the 458-task setting. Equal-budget controls show that diagnostic value depends on model-task configuration: command feedback improves 3B from 312/458 to 361/458, whereas generic rejection outperforms the diagnostic for 14B As filters, schema and syntax/build gates leave invalid fractions among accepted artifacts of 42.2% and 40.1%, whereas execution/test gates reduce this fraction to 2.0%. The strict alternative retains no reference-invalid artifact but rejects 17.0% of reference-valid artifacts. Conclusion: Quality gates should be reported as costed interventions rather than hidden components of aggregate agent scores. Gate reports should distinguish filtering, diagnostic content, and retry opportunity. Feedback value depends on model capability and repairability as well as prompt and task context; stricter filtering can reject valid outputs.
