KW  - Code refactoring
KW  - Large language models
KW  - Multi-agent systems
KW  - Retrieval-augmented generation
KW  - Software maintenance
KW  - Github automation
AB  - While recent agentic coding tools have extended Large Language Model capabilities to repository-level tasks, their closed-source architectures preclude independent evaluation of pipeline components, cross-model comparison, and detection of self-evaluation bias. This paper presents RepoAI, an open, model-agnostic multi-agent system that automates repository-level code refactoring through coordinated LLM orchestration with cross-family validation. Our approach employs specialised agents for intent classification, code generation, and validation, working collaboratively to handle complex multi-file modifications. The system combines Retrieval-Augmented Generation for context-aware code understanding with a comprehensive validation pipeline that uses cross-family LLM judges to eliminate self-evaluation bias. We evaluate RepoAI on 1404 benchmark tasks using RefactorBench [1] (100 tasks from 10+ open-source projects), comparing five pipeline conditions across seven generator models from four model families. A Friedman test confirms statistically significant differences among conditions (chi(2) = 11.08, p = 0.026), with pairwise Wilcoxon signed-rank tests showing that the full multi-agent pipeline significantly outperforms direct generation (p = 0.007). Multi-model evaluation demonstrates generalisability across Qwen, Devstral, Llama 4, and Gemma 2, while a systematic error taxonomy reveals that all 170 observed failures stem from model-specific generation errors rather than pipeline deficiencies.
